---
import { PRODUCT_MODELS, PRODUCT_MODEL_INFO, PLAN_LIMITS } from '@golem/shared';
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE } from '../../lib/studio-plugin';
const free = PLAN_LIMITS.free;
---
<DocsLayout
  title="Apple and Apple MAX — Apple Docs"
  description="Choose Apple for limited free use or Apple MAX with an eligible paid subscription. Model choice is separate from permission to change your Studio place."
  heading="Apple and Apple MAX"
>
  <p>The selector beside the message box chooses a product model, not a planning mode.</p>
  {PRODUCT_MODELS.map(model => <section>
    <h2>{PRODUCT_MODEL_INFO[model].name}</h2>
    {/*
      THE STEP CEILING WAS NOWHERE A READER COULD FIND IT BEFORE SIGNING UP.
      A run on the free Apple lane is clamped to three steps — `maxStepsFor` in the worker takes the
      minimum of the mode's limit and Clay's — and /pricing sells the allowance in "quality-gated
      builds", a unit its own FAQ defines as read the tree, edit scripts, create instances, render,
      critique. That is five steps before the fix. The compute the page counts is genuinely
      delivered and the build genuinely completes, but it completes across several messages, and the
      only place that shape was ever stated was a "Step 2 of 3" counter during the run itself.
      Stated here and on the Free card; apps/site/tests/free-lane-steps.test.mjs reads the worker's
      own constant and fails when the two disagree.
    */}
    {model === 'apple'
      ? <p>Apple is the limited free model. It can answer questions and use building tools when Studio is connected and changes are allowed. It is not a read-only Plan mode. The Free plan includes {free.creditsPerDay} Credits per day, subject to its monthly allowance. It works in short bursts: <strong>one request runs at most three steps</strong>, then stops and says so with everything it did saved — send another message and it picks up where it left off. A build that needs more than three steps is not refused; it takes more than one message.</p>
      : <p>Apple MAX is available to eligible paid subscribers and carries a longer chain of steps in a single request, so an involved build can finish without being handed back to you partway. Choosing it on a free account shows an availability notice and keeps your draft; it does not switch your model or charge you. See <a href="/app/usage">Usage and plan availability</a>.</p>}
  </section>)}
  <h2>Availability</h2>
  <p>Paid checkout is not open in the current preview. Check <a href="/app/usage">Usage and plan availability</a> before expecting access to MAX. Extra purchased Credits do not by themselves grant a paid subscription.</p>
  <h2>What changes in Studio</h2>
  <p>Model access and edit permission are separate. Neither choice bypasses Studio pairing, change approval, quota, or tool safety checks. Without a connected plugin, a chat response is not a change to your place. {!STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE && <>Public plugin installation is currently unavailable. </>}See <a href="/docs/plugin">installation status</a>.</p>
  <h2>How Credits work</h2>
  <p>There is no guaranteed fixed charge per message. Actual usage and the plan limits determine the charge. See <a href="/docs/credits-and-limits">Credits and limits</a>; review the result in Studio before treating a build as complete.</p>
  {/*
    "They currently share a foundation model" was true when it was written and is not true now.
    The worker's gateway maps Apple to one third-party model and Apple MAX to another; they have
    been different since MAX moved lanes. The sentence was doing honest work — denying that either
    is a bespoke Roblox model — so it is corrected rather than deleted, and the test beside this
    page reads both model ids out of the gateway so the correction cannot go stale the same way.
  */}
  <h2>Training status</h2>
  <p>Apple and Apple MAX are separate product choices with different access and working limits, and they run on different third-party foundation models — neither is a model we trained. Custom Roblox training is in development; a completed independently trained frontier model is not available.</p>
</DocsLayout>
